{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013kWcmEunK7Xdy5CpH1jM8x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"TCP/IP traces its origins to the early 1970s, when Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn began designing a protocol suite to interconnect the disparate packet-switched networks emerging from ARPANET research. Their seminal 1974 paper, \"A Protocol for Packet Network Intercommunication,\" introduced the foundational concepts that would later be split into the Transmission Control Protocol and the Internet Protocol. On January 1, 1983, ARPANET officially transitioned from the older NCP to TCP/IP in an event often called \"flag day,\" cementing the suite as the backbone of what would become the modern Internet. Over the following decades, TCP/IP evolved through standardization by the IETF, the introduction of IPv6 to address exhaustion concerns, and continual refinement to support the global, multi-billion-device network we rely on today."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9293,"cache_read_input_tokens":18921,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9293},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4dcccc5f-f2f2-4400-a37e-2c6d3fde03a2","uuid":"7a07d2c0-1549-446f-ad86-b0b1957afe65"}
